  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 400 AM EDT SAT AUG 22 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, the main story will be pleasant\nweather more reminiscent of early fall than late August. Upper \nlevel troughing will sit over the eastern CONUS, with a relatively\nvertically stacked low pressure system over Ontario. Meanwhile, \nto the southwest of the forecast area, upper level ridging will be\ncentered over the Southern High Plains. At the surface, high \npressure will sit over the Midwest, with winds generally light and\nnorthwesterly across eastern Kentucky; this will work to maintain\nthe cooler and drier postfrontal air mass to begin the week. \nThese light winds and a weak pressure gradient across the area \nwill also allow for a modest ridge valley temperature split \novernight Sunday into Monday morning, with typical valley fog \nformation. MOS guidance could be a bit aggressive with overnight \nlows for some spots, given lingering moisture, and low 50s for the\ncoldest spots may be overdone. For most or all locations, low \ntemperatures will likely range from the mid 50s in valley \nlocations to the upper 50s or around 60 on the ridgetops. During \nthe day on Monday, the upper level ridge will expand eastward \nsomewhat. Associated height rises across the Ohio Valley should \nwork to keep the forecast area dry, with temperatures rising into \nthe upper 70s to low 80s. Thankfully, continued northwesterly \nwinds will keep dewpoints blissfully low in the mid 50s. \nOvernight, low temperatures will again fall into the 50s, with a \nsimilar modest ridge valley temperature split and another night of\nriver valley fog.\n\nOn Tuesday, forecast uncertainty increases somewhat. The upper level\ntrough axis will be well east of the area, with eastern Kentucky \nin a region of northwest flow aloft. Many models depict a \nshortwave traversing this flow, with fairly substantial spatial \nand timing differences; at present, the most likely outcome is \nthat, at best, this energy delivers a glancing blow to the \nforecast area, relegating any rain chances to Tennessee. However, \nsome deterministic model runs do suggest that an isolated shower \nor storm could be possible in our southwestern counties. Even so, \na drier solution currently appears far more likely, and NBM PoPs \nare accordingly very low.\n\nMore widespread rain chances may return to the forecast on\nWednesday as a surface low originally over Oklahoma translates\nnortheastward. A warm front associated with this low will attempt\nto lift towards the area; however, a cold front extending from\nanother surface low over the Upper Midwest will loom to the west\nof the forecast area. Given the expected eastward progression of\nthis cold front, there is significant uncertainty regarding how\nfar north the warm front can progress before it is overtaken.\nBroadly, Wednesday and Thursday will feature increased moisture\nand a chance of showers and storms; however, any forecast details\nwill remain dependent on the location, progression, and timing of\nthese boundaries, and forecast uncertainty is very high at this\ntime.",
